&lt;h2&gt;


  What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I often wake up to hundreds of unread messages across family, friends, and apartment group chats.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most are greetings, stickers, forwards, cricket debates, and parking arguments. Somewhere in that noise, however, Mom may have asked me to call or tonight’s plan may have moved to Saturday.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built &lt;strong&gt;SleepIn&lt;/strong&gt; so I can find those two important messages without reading the other 500.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Drop in WhatsApp or Telegram chat exports, enter your name, and SleepIn produces a private morning digest such as:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rahul’s birthday moved to Saturday, Mom wants you to call, nothing else matters.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The result is divided into:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What matters:&lt;/strong&gt; Direct requests, changed plans, deadlines, money, health, and family updates.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What can wait:&lt;/strong&gt; Everything that does not require my attention.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every result includes the relevant group name. Senders retain the name—or unsaved phone number—found in the original export.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is essentially a permission slip to mute every group chat and sleep peacefully.&lt;/p&gt;

