&lt;h1 class="heading-element"&gt;SleepIn&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I'm in a family group, a friends group and a society group, and every morning there are a few hundred unread messages waiting. Usually only two of them matter. So I made SleepIn: you give it last night's WhatsApp or Telegram export, a small open-weight model running on your own laptop reads everything, and you get something like:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Rahul's birthday moved to Saturday, mom wants you to call, nothing else matters.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/neoquantx/SleepIn/docs/digest.png"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fneoquantx%2FSleepIn%2FHEAD%2Fdocs%2Fdigest.png" alt="SleepIn digest for the sample chats"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Everything runs on your machine. The chat file is parsed in the browser and the summary comes from Ollama on localhost. No cloud API, no login, no analytics (Next.js telemetry is switched off in the npm scripts too). That was the whole point. I'm not uploading my mom's messages anywhere.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Made for Week 3 of the Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge, "Slow and Steady" (International Sloth Day). The model does the catching up overnight, you stay in bed.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="markdown-heading"&gt;
&lt;h2 class="heading-element"&gt;Running&lt;/h2&gt;…&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="gh-btn-container"&gt;&lt;a class="gh-btn" href="https://github.com/neoquantx/SleepIn" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;View on GitHub&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;h2&gt;


  How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SleepIn uses &lt;strong&gt;Next.js 16&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;TypeScript&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;Tailwind CSS&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;shadcn/ui&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;Ollama&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The processing flow is intentionally simple:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The user exports a WhatsApp &lt;code&gt;.txt&lt;/code&gt; file or Telegram &lt;code&gt;result.json&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;SleepIn parses the export and filters the selected time window.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A local open-weight model reads each group separately.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The app combines only the important results into one short digest.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;WhatsApp exports differ between Android and iPhone, so I created a parser that handles both formats, multiline messages, 12/24-hour timestamps, media placeholders, deleted messages, and system notices. The Telegram parser supports individual chats and full-account JSON exports.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Why one chat at a time?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first version sent every group to the model at once. A small 3B model sometimes mixed up conversations, treated jokes as important, or attached a message to the wrong group.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The improved version processes one group at a time and requests structured JSON containing:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A short headline&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;An importance score&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Items that matter&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Items that can wait&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is slower, but significantly more reliable—which fits the &lt;strong&gt;Slow and Steady&lt;/strong&gt; theme perfectly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also added guardrails around the model:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Names and places absent from the source chat are rejected.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A group needs evidence such as a direct mention, request, or changed plan before reaching the main digest.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Later messages override earlier plans.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If Ollama is unavailable, demo mode still lets people try the complete experience.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SleepIn defaults to &lt;code&gt;llama3.2&lt;/code&gt;, while also supporting locally installed models such as Qwen, Gemma, Mistral, and Phi.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Private group conversations can contain phone numbers, home addresses, health updates, financial details, and family information. Uploading all of that to a closed cloud API did not feel responsible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With Ollama and open-weight models, SleepIn can run on the user’s own computer:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Chat files are parsed locally.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Inference runs through Ollama on &lt;code&gt;localhost&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No messages are sent to a cloud AI provider.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;There are no API keys, subscriptions, accounts, or per-token charges.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Users can change the model instead of being locked to one provider.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A cloud model might respond faster, but this project does not need instant output. Taking around a minute to privately process hundreds of overnight messages is a trade-off I am happy to make.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In SleepIn, being slow is not a limitation. It is part of the privacy model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The Lazy Payoff
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I created three reproducible sample conversations representing a realistic morning inbox:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A friends group where Rahul’s birthday moves to Saturday&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A family group where Mom asks for a call&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A residents group full of parking arguments and a temporarily lost cat&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Together, they contain roughly &lt;strong&gt;520 messages&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reading them manually could take 10–15 minutes—and I might still miss the final venue change. SleepIn processes them while I make coffee and returns the two lines that deserve my attention.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the payoff: less scrolling, fewer missed plans, and a few extra minutes in bed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Prize Categories
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Overall Challenge:&lt;/strong&gt; SleepIn is entered for the overall prize of &lt;strong&gt;$250 USD, DEV++ membership, and the exclusive winner badge&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Partner Categories:&lt;/strong&gt; Github Colipot .&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every proxy and IP vendor on the internet uses the word "residential" now. Residential datacenter. Residential-grade. True residential. Meanwhile, half the "residential" IPs people buy are recycled datacenter addresses wearing a costume — and the platforms you log into can tell the difference in about one second.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The good news: you don't have to take the vendor's word for it. With three free tools and five minutes, you can verify what an IP actually is, where it really lives, and whether it's already burned. This is the exact checklist I run before any IP touches an important account.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  First, understand what you're actually checking
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"Residential" is not a vibe. Technically, it means three verifiable things:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The ASN belongs to a consumer ISP&lt;/strong&gt; — the kind of network that sells home broadband — not a hosting company.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The IP is not flagged as hosting, proxy, or VPN&lt;/strong&gt; in the major IP intelligence databases.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The IP has a clean (or at least neutral) reputation&lt;/strong&gt; — no blacklist history, no high fraud score.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If any one of the three fails, the IP is not what was advertised, no matter what the sales page says.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 1: ipinfo.io — who owns this IP, really?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open &lt;code&gt;ipinfo.io/&amp;lt;your-ip&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; (or run &lt;code&gt;curl ipinfo.io/&amp;lt;your-ip&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Read these fields:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;org&lt;/code&gt; / ASN.&lt;/strong&gt; This is the big one. You want to see a consumer-facing ISP — the kind of name you'd see on a home internet bill. If the org is a cloud provider, a hosting company, or anything with "Data Center," "Hosting," "Cloud," or "LLC" (in the hosting sense) in the name, it is a datacenter IP. Full stop.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;privacy&lt;/code&gt; flags&lt;/strong&gt; (on ipinfo's privacy data): &lt;code&gt;hosting: true&lt;/code&gt; is an instant fail for a "residential" claim. &lt;code&gt;proxy: true&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;vpn: true&lt;/code&gt; means the databases have already classified it — platforms see the same data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Location consistency.&lt;/strong&gt; City, region, and timezone should agree with each other. An IP that geolocates to one state while its timezone says another is a sign of sloppy or spoofed registration data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pro tip: check the whole neighborhood, not just your address. If you're assigned 203.0.113.47, look up .1 and .100 from the same range. If the neighbors are all flagged hosting IPs, your "residential" address is a house drawn on a warehouse.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 2: ping0.cc — the China-community lie detector
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ping0.cc is a favorite in the Chinese developer and cross-border community for a reason: it aggregates several databases and gives a blunt risk verdict.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Paste the IP and look at:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;IP type attribution&lt;/strong&gt; — it will plainly label the address as residential broadband, datacenter, or business line, based on the owning network.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Risk / purity readout&lt;/strong&gt; — it cross-references multiple sources, so a discrepancy between what ipinfo says and what ping0 says is itself a signal worth investigating.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Native vs. broadcast IP&lt;/strong&gt; — for streaming and region-locked services this matters: a "US IP" that is actually announced from another country (a broadcast IP) will fail region checks that a native IP passes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 3: Scamalytics — how burned is it?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Scamalytics assigns every IP a &lt;strong&gt;fraud score from 0 to 100&lt;/strong&gt;, and this (or something very like it) is what risk engines at major platforms are effectively computing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;0–10:&lt;/strong&gt; Clean. Normal for a genuine household connection.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;11–30:&lt;/strong&gt; Usable, but look closer — maybe a business line or a range with some history.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;31–60:&lt;/strong&gt; Risky. Expect extra verification challenges, CAPTCHAs, and scrutiny.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;60+:&lt;/strong&gt; Burned. Don't put a valuable account on it; you'll spend your life in verification loops.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Check the detail flags too: "Server" = datacenter, "Public Proxy" and "Tor Exit Node" are automatic disqualifications, and a history of appearing on blacklists (check via a multi-RBL lookup if Scamalytics hints at it) means previous tenants abused the address.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also run IPs through lumaoai.com/ip-check, which bundles the same core signals — ISP type, hosting/proxy flags, and risk level — into one quick verdict page. Use whichever tool you like; just don't skip the step because a vendor said "trust me."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 3.5: Cross-check when the databases disagree
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The tools won't always agree, and the disagreements are informative rather than confusing once you know how to read them:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ipinfo says residential, Scamalytics says high risk.&lt;/strong&gt; The ASN is a genuine ISP, but the specific range has abuse history — think of a budget ISP whose addresses get recycled through heavy prior use. Verdict: residential but &lt;em&gt;dirty&lt;/em&gt;. Fine for low-stakes use, wrong for a flagship account.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ping0 says datacenter, vendor swears it's residential.&lt;/strong&gt; Trust the databases. Some vendors buy hosting ranges that are &lt;em&gt;registered&lt;/em&gt; through an ISP-looking shell. The routing and the database consensus give it away.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Everything is clean except one blacklist hit from two years ago.&lt;/strong&gt; Old, single, minor listings (like a dynamic-pool policy list) are common on real residential ranges and usually harmless. Recent hits on abuse lists are not.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Databases also update at different speeds. An IP that was re-registered from hosting to ISP use can carry stale flags for weeks. If every signal is clean except one lagging database, re-check in a few days — but don't deploy a valuable account on it in the meantime.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 4: The stability test nobody does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A genuinely residential, genuinely &lt;em&gt;dedicated&lt;/em&gt; IP has one more property: &lt;strong&gt;it doesn't change.&lt;/strong&gt; Connect through the IP, note the exit address, disconnect, reconnect an hour later and again the next day. If the exit IP rotates, you're on a shared or rotating pool regardless of what you paid for — and rotating exits are one of the fastest ways to trigger account security reviews. A real static residential IP resolves to the same address every single time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Keep a record, and re-check monthly
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Save your results — a screenshot or a note with the date, the ASN, the flags, and the fraud score — for every IP you deploy. Two reasons. First, if an account gets challenged later, your records tell you whether the IP's reputation changed or whether the problem is behavioral. Second, IP reputation is not permanent: ranges get resold, neighbors get abusive, databases reclassify. A five-minute re-check once a month on the IPs your important accounts live on catches a degrading address while you can still migrate calmly, instead of during a ban appeal.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Red flags cheat sheet
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;ASN is a hosting/cloud company → not residential&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;hosting: true&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;proxy: true&lt;/code&gt; in any major database → not clean&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fraud score above 30 → expect friction; above 60 → walk away&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Exit IP changes between sessions → not static, not dedicated&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Geolocation and timezone disagree → spoofed or misregistered&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The seller can't tell you which ISP the range belongs to → they don't know, or they do and won't say&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why this matters more than your setup
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;People obsess over browser fingerprints, user agents, and warm-up routines — all of which matter — while logging in from an IP that three public databases have already flagged as a proxy. Platforms build their risk models on exactly this data because it's cheap to check and hard to fake. A residential IP from a real consumer ISP, with hosting/proxy flags off and a low fraud score, is the foundation everything else sits on.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's why, when I source IPs for account work (the static residential addresses at lumaozhanghao.com are the ones I test with this exact checklist), the rule is simple: verify first, log in second. Five minutes of checking beats five weeks of ban appeals.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;“Adapt or die.” That phrase perfectly sums up the unstable software engineering industry in this age of AI. Whenever we set aside the fear of AI taking our jobs, we’re often met with the same reassurance: AI isn’t replacing developers; developers who know how to leverage AI will come out on top.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And sure, that sounds reasonable for senior developers with years of experience and solid skills to back them up. But what about junior and entry-level developers, myself included? How do I possibly leverage AI to become a better software engineer when the foundation hasn’t been built yet?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Learning to Code in a Different Era
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before I start, it’s probably best to offer some context. I’m a full-stack developer currently focused on web development, though I plan to expand my skill set down the road. I’m still an entry-level/junior dev, and my only “working experience” so far comes from a remote internship I recently completed at a somewhat shady company.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now, it should be obvious that I still have plenty of room to grow, both in terms of my technical skills and actual work experience. But that’s exactly the problem. With how volatile the industry is right now, thanks to AI and the aftermath of post-COVID overhiring (and now layoffs), being at the very bottom of the software development career ladder isn’t exactly a great position.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Despite all this, I’m still learning to code manually. According to many experts, though, coding by hand is dead. What matters now is having “taste,” making high-level decisions, and understanding trade-offs while letting AI handle the “boring” part: implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That sounds great. Until you consider that those skills and that judgment come from years of learning and experience, often gained on the job. You can’t reliably judge an AI’s code quality or spot its flaws when you don’t yet understand what good code looks like.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Using an AI Assistant Changes Everything
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Whether for better or worse, it's clear that AI has changed everything. I'm not particularly against artificial intelligence (GenAI, to be specific), though I'm quite aware of its numerous downsides. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When it comes to learning to code, I actively use AI as a learning tool. It helps me break down obscure concepts, provides coding challenges, and even points me toward useful resources. Whenever I get stuck on a problem or confused while debugging my code, getting help is just a click or prompt away. And that, in itself, is the problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've come to realize that solving problems doesn't automatically make you a better programmer. The friction involved in solving them matters just as much. Taking a problem, working through it, deeply thinking, making mistakes, and eventually finding a solution helps develop the problem-solving skills software engineers need.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But with a handy assistant always within reach, I've made it incredibly easy to bypass that friction, even when it's exactly what I need. It's like being stuck in tutorial hell, except somehow worse. I get a false sense of progress that looks solid at a cursory glance but crumbles when put under real pressure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What Happens When the AI Isn't There?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Outsourcing your intelligence to AI is one of the worst things to do. Letting tools like ChatGPT or Claude handle our thinking, problem-solving, and decision-making actively weakens our “mental muscles” due to lack of use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With AI at my side, I sometimes solve problems without giving them much thought. I accept its answers without much questions, and that makes things harder when the assistant isn't there. I start overthinking, doubting things I genuinely know, and struggling to recall even basic concepts I haven't practiced in a while.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A quick search led me to the concept of &lt;em&gt;cognitive laziness&lt;/em&gt;: the tendency to avoid putting in mental effort when an easier alternative is available. Over-relying on AI can encourage this habit, particularly when we let it do the thinking instead of using it to support our own. It’s a horrible cycle. The more we depend on AI to solve problems, the less practice we get solving them independently, which can make us even more dependent on AI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In my own case, I sometimes struggle to explain code I've written with AI's help. Adapting it to changing requirements can be difficult because, while I understand the overall idea, the actual implementation feels foreign to me.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  I Still Want to Understand the Code I Write
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most developers who embrace the AI boom argue that it “lets them skip the boring part of typing and focus on high-level decisions.” Well, I actually love what they refer to as the boring part. Thinking through problems, typing out code to solve them, and simply enjoying the logic feels great to me.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So, even if it makes me feel slower in the short term, I'll try to avoid reliance on AI tools. For now, I'll attempt to solve problems myself, thinking deeply when I'm stuck, rather than relying on some tool, and take the time to understand an implementation instead of blindly accepting it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Using AI to speed up as a shortcut feels like strapping a rocket to a horse-drawn carriage. Sure, it'll go faster, but how long before the whole thing falls apart? And if that becomes the norm, I’d have to ask whether I’m still learning software development or signing up to supervise AI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Short answer:&lt;/strong&gt; use error tracking for failed code, uptime monitoring for reachable services, and a cron heartbeat for scheduled work that never started or finished.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a small edtech SaaS comparing an experiment across tenant cohorts, the heartbeat is the decisive addition. A green API and an empty error inbox cannot prove that last night's aggregation ran. If the job emits a completion heartbeat only after it commits attributed usage to Postgres, silence becomes evidence rather than an absence of evidence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Signal&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Question it answers&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Useful cohort evidence&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Blind spot&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Error tracking&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Did executed code fail?&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Exception plus tenant and experiment context&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A scheduler that never invokes the code&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Uptime probe&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Can a known path answer now?&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;API and dependency availability&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A healthy web process with a missing background run&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cron heartbeat&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Did expected work report by its deadline?&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Run ID, cohort counts, and completion time&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Correctness inside a run unless the payload is validated&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Recommendation:&lt;/strong&gt; keep all three, but page on the signal closest to the promise being made. Probe the learner-facing path for availability, capture exceptions from executed code, and require a completion heartbeat for the nightly cohort rollup. Do not make one signal impersonate the other two.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How should error tracking, uptime monitoring, and a cron heartbeat differ?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An uptime check makes a request on a schedule and judges the response. That is useful evidence that the selected path can respond. It says nothing about a separate scheduler unless that request exercises the scheduler itself. A health response can be green while the nightly aggregation has not run.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Error tracking starts later in the causal chain. The runtime must execute instrumented code and encounter a captured failure. If a deployment omits the scheduler entry, a queue trigger is disabled, or the process exits before installing its handlers, there may be no application exception to report. No execution, no captured error.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the silent-failure boundary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nothing crashed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A heartbeat reverses the test. Instead of waiting for a failure event, monitoring waits for evidence of success within an expected window. For the experiment job, a start event proves only that execution began. A completion event sent after the database transaction proves more: this run reached the point at which its attributed records were committed. It still does not prove the numbers are correct, so validation remains part of the job.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This distinction matters to a solo operator because every alert spends attention. I would rather define one narrow promise per signal than debug a vague "background processing looks odd" alarm between feature releases. Ship weekly, but make the scheduled promises explicit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One promise. One deadline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Put evidence after the business invariant
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The heartbeat position should follow the invariant, not the last convenient line in the scheduler wrapper. For this edtech experiment, the invariant might be: every eligible tenant cohort has one idempotent aggregate for a particular experiment date, and the usage assigned to those rows reconciles with the run total.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Send a start event if run duration is operationally useful. Send completion only after validation and commit. If validation fails, throw an error with the run identifier and structured cohort context; do not send completion. Error tracking then explains the executed failure, while the missing completion independently detects that the scheduled promise was missed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is a trap here. A &lt;code&gt;finally&lt;/code&gt; block feels reliable, so it is tempting to put the success heartbeat there. But &lt;code&gt;finally&lt;/code&gt; runs after both success and failure. That turns a failed aggregation into apparent success. The correct location is deliberately boring: after the commit, after reconciliation, and outside the failure path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Placement wins.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The identifiers need stable meanings. A &lt;code&gt;run_id&lt;/code&gt; identifies one attempted execution. An &lt;code&gt;experiment_id&lt;/code&gt; identifies the comparison. A pseudonymous &lt;code&gt;tenant_id&lt;/code&gt; or cohort key supports attribution without putting student data into telemetry. W3C Trace Context defines a standard format for propagating trace identity across process boundaries; OpenTelemetry's semantic conventions provide common names for telemetry attributes. Neither standard decides which business dimensions are safe to collect. That remains an application decision.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Attribute cost where work is accepted
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A cohort experiment is not useful if the result says treatment beat control but the cost ledger cannot say which tenants produced the work. Cost attribution should therefore be recorded beside the durable aggregate, using the same run and experiment identifiers. Logs alone are a weak ledger: retention can differ, retries duplicate events, and a query over messages does not automatically share the transaction that accepted the result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use an append-only usage record with an idempotency key. The actual unit can be tokens, task executions, storage bytes, or another measurable driver. Do not force unlike units into a fictional dollar value inside the observability layer. Finance rules and provider rates can change; raw quantities plus an effective timestamp preserve the inputs needed to recalculate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The decision rule is simple. &lt;strong&gt;Alert on missing evidence, but compare cohorts from durable, reconciled records.&lt;/strong&gt; Monitoring tells you whether to investigate. Postgres holds the data used for the experiment and its cost attribution.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Alerts are not ledgers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This also controls cardinality. Tenant and experiment dimensions are valuable on a bounded run record, but copying every learner or request identifier into metric labels can create an unbounded series set. Keep high-cardinality detail in traces, structured events, or database rows with explicit retention and access rules. Use metrics for bounded operational dimensions such as job name, environment, and result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A TypeScript completion boundary
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This sketch uses generic interfaces. The quantities are application measurements, not prices. The important part is the order: calculate, validate, commit the aggregate and usage record, then emit completion.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Cohort&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;control&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;treatment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CohortResult&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;tenantId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;experimentId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;cohort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Cohort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;eligibleLearners&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;completedLessons&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;computeMillis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kr"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ExperimentStore&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;saveRun&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;runId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;results&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CohortResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[];&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;usage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Array&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;idempotencyKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nl"&gt;tenantId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nl"&gt;experimentId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nl"&gt;unit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;compute_ms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nl"&gt;quantity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kr"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Signals&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;started&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;runId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;completed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;runId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cohortCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;capture&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;aggregateExperiment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;runId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;experimentId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;store&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ExperimentStore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;signals&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Signals&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;calculate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;CohortResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;signals&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;started&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;runId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;results&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;calculate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tenants&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;results&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tenantId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;results&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tenants&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;size&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Experiment aggregation produced no tenant cohorts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;store&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;saveRun&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;runId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;results&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;usage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;results&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="na"&gt;idempotencyKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;runId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tenantId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cohort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="na"&gt;tenantId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tenantId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nx"&gt;experimentId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="na"&gt;unit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;compute_ms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="na"&gt;quantity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;computeMillis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;})),&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;signals&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;completed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;runId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;results&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;signals&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;capture&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;runId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;experimentId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;saveRun&lt;/code&gt; should implement one database transaction and reject duplicate idempotency keys. The heartbeat adapter should use a short timeout and bounded retry policy. Decide failure semantics explicitly: if the data committed but heartbeat delivery fails, retrying the whole aggregation must not double-charge usage. Idempotency makes that recovery path tolerable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Notice what the code does not do. It does not send tenant IDs to the heartbeat channel. A cohort count is enough to diagnose missing or suspicious completion at that layer; authorized operators can join the &lt;code&gt;runId&lt;/code&gt; to the durable records when deeper inspection is warranted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That boundary is intentional.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Test the boundaries, not just the happy function. Run one test where &lt;code&gt;calculate&lt;/code&gt; throws, one where the transaction rejects, one where completion delivery times out, and one where the same &lt;code&gt;runId&lt;/code&gt; is replayed. Then deploy a canary schedule with a short-lived test job and verify that removing its completion signal produces an alert after the configured grace period.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  When is the runner-up signal enough?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A heartbeat is unnecessary for work that has no independent schedule or completion promise. If a learner's request synchronously performs the whole operation, an uptime probe against that representative path plus error tracking may cover the relevant boundaries. The probe exercises availability; captured exceptions explain failures. Adding a cron contract where no cron exists creates noise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This design has limits. A heartbeat cannot prove statistical validity, detect biased cohort assignment, or explain an exception, and a completion-only contract is a poor fit for continuously running consumers with no discrete deadline. For those consumers, measure queue age or lag instead. For immediate learner-facing availability, choose a representative uptime probe. For an executed code failure, choose error tracking with enough structured context to diagnose it. These are trade-offs between evidence types, not interchangeable product features.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Uptime monitoring is the better primary alert when the user-facing promise is immediate reachability. A completed nightly job does not prove that the lesson API can answer now. Conversely, error tracking should be the primary diagnostic signal for a thrown exception with stack and request context. A heartbeat can say the deadline passed, but it cannot reconstruct the failing line.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The signals overlap on purpose, yet their evidence differs. Keep the overlap when the consequence of silence is material. For a low-stakes cleanup task, a database timestamp checked during routine operations may be enough. For a cohort experiment that informs rollout and allocates tenant cost, delayed discovery can contaminate the decision, so an explicit completion deadline earns its maintenance cost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Operate the contract without building a monitoring project
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with one scheduled promise: the aggregation must complete by a defined time after its schedule. Add a grace period based on normal runtime variability and upstream dependencies, not a round number copied from another service. Document who receives the alert, what run record to inspect, and how replay preserves idempotency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then measure the monitoring path itself. A synthetic canary heartbeat can verify that missing-signal detection and notification still work. Review stale schedules after deployment changes; an intentionally retired job should have its heartbeat contract retired in the same change. Otherwise the monitoring layer teaches the operator to ignore alerts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep this small. The revenue-per-hour lens favors a generic &lt;code&gt;Signals&lt;/code&gt; port, a transactional store, and managed or self-hosted adapters chosen on operational fit. The adapter is undifferentiated plumbing. Outsource it when that buys back feature time, but retain the evidence model and identifiers in application code so a tool change does not rewrite the business invariant.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The final check is concrete: can one &lt;code&gt;runId&lt;/code&gt; connect the schedule expectation, captured failure, committed cohort outputs, and raw usage quantities without exposing learner data? If yes, the three signals form a useful chain. If not, another dashboard will not repair the missing relationship.&lt;/p&gt;

